on room at six fifteen, someone was already there. Someone younger, it seemed.\nShe couldn’t make out the face behind the blond hair. But the body was fine enough, leaning back on the couch with bare legs crossed and arms flung in opposite directions, one palm pressing the padded wall and the other wrist resting on the low table beside him.\nShe stood stock-still by the doorway for a moment, aware of her breath and of his awareness that she was here. He stirred and tilted his head; a face turned toward her, expressionless.\n\nThe two pairs of eyes held each other through an entire exhale.\nAnd then the young man had pushed himself up on the armrests, stretched to his full height, and strolled over to shake her hand.\n“Mara?”\nHe was closer than she would have guessed from where he’d been sitting, broad-shouldered and strong-chinned in a white T-shirt, grinning at her through dark-framed glasses as he reached out for the handshake. She took it.\n“Jonah,” he said.\n“Hello,” she replied, and then they stood there, still shaking hands, not knowing what to do next.\n\nAnd that was when a door opened down the hallway behind her, releasing two voices into the room. One female, one male; one asking a question, the other replying; neither loud nor excited, just casual conversation. Jonah released her hand and turned away to look down the hall at them.\nWhen Mara heard the voices and followed his gaze, she saw one young man who wasn’t Jonah, but close in size and shape; he had brown hair and a beard and was carrying a glass.\n\nThe brown-haired man glanced once over his shoulder, saw them standing there, nodded hello, and stepped right into the gathering, holding up his glass of wine—a half-full plastic cup with the Fulcrum logo on it. “Cheers.” He set it on the table against the wall to the left of the door, and stepped forward to shake Jonah’s hand as they exchanged names.\n\nAs he set down the glass and moved out of its space, a previously unseen catering worker entered from the hallway behind him and retrieved it. She wore the same black-and-white striped Fulcrum uniform shirt, though she looked old enough to have had that job since the Institute was founded; and she gave no outward sign of the social fact that she was taking their discarded drink back to the kitchen, while the rest of the conversation went on.\n\nMara watched this woman return to the hallway where she belonged, and noticed herself noticing who assumed whose labor.","text_hash":"f64a82a705f76bab9972f18217b8b1cf5828db3cde9ebbe51d4461231082dec9","timings":{"cache_n":3683,"predicted_ms":16939.775,"predicted_n":113,"predicted_per_second":6.670690726411655,"predicted_per_token_ms":149.9095132743363,"prompt_ms":173.56,"prompt_n":1,"prompt_per_second":5.7616962433740495,"prompt_per_token_ms":173.56},"usage":{"completion_tokens":113,"prompt_tokens":3684},"version":"gemma-fiction-autoresearch.v4"}
